A socket file is not a running daemon
===================================

The arch series failed its own suite:

    test tests::cursor_position_is_not_claimed_until_known ... FAILED
    panicked at crates/quixi-platform/src/lib.rs:2203

The test is right. It asks the capability report whether input synthesis is available, and if so,
performs a move and asserts the position is now known. On this box `input_synthesis` came back `true`
while every synthesized event failed, which is precisely the contradiction the test was written to
catch.

The cause is `ydotool_socket()`: it probed candidates with `Path::exists()`, and a unix socket leaves
its inode behind when the process behind it dies. So "a socket file is here" was being read as
"something will answer", the capability report said input was available, and the move went into a
dead socket.

`exists()` is now a connect. A local unix connect either completes or is refused immediately, so it
costs nothing and it cannot claim a live daemon that is not there. Same treatment for the
`YDOTOOL_SOCKET` override, which had the identical flaw.
diff -Naur -x target /tmp/archbefore/crates/quixi-platform/src/linux.rs /tmp/archpatched/crates/quixi-platform/src/linux.rs
--- a/crates/quixi-platform/src/linux.rs
+++ b/crates/quixi-platform/src/linux.rs
@@ -1514,17 +1514,33 @@
 /// the code never set `YDOTOOL_SOCKET` the client looked in the wrong place and everything
 /// failed while the daemon sat there running. Honour the environment first, then look in the
 /// places a daemon actually binds.
+/// Is there actually a daemon listening on this path?
+///
+/// A unix socket leaves its inode behind when the process dies, so `exists()` answers "a socket file
+/// is here", not "something will accept a connection". Those are different claims, and confusing them
+/// is how the capability report ended up advertising input synthesis on a box where every synthesized
+/// event failed: the test that caught it moves the pointer, asks whether the position is now known,
+/// and gets "yes it should be" from the capability probe and "no" from reality.
+///
+/// Connect, then drop. Cheap enough to do per probe, and it cannot lie about a live daemon.
+fn socket_is_live(path: &std::path::Path) -> bool {
+    // `UnixStream` has no connect_timeout, and does not need one: a local unix connect either
+    // completes or is refused immediately, with no network round trip to wait on.
+    use std::os::unix::net::UnixStream;
+    UnixStream::connect(path).is_ok()
+}
+
 fn ydotool_socket() -> Option<std::path::PathBuf> {
-    // Only honour YDOTOOL_SOCKET when the socket is actually there.
+    // Only honour YDOTOOL_SOCKET when a daemon is actually answering there.
     //
     // Trusting it blindly is how input dies silently: a launcher, a systemd unit or a test harness
-    // exports a path it believes is right, the path does not exist, and every call fails with an
-    // empty error because the message only says "failed". Measured on a box whose ydotoold is the
-    // distro service on /run/ydotoold.socket while the caller had exported
+    // exports a path it believes is right, nothing is listening, and every call fails with an empty
+    // error because the message only says "failed". Measured on a box whose ydotoold is the distro
+    // service on /run/ydotoold.socket while the caller had exported
     // $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/.ydotool_socket. Fall through to the probe instead.
     if let Some(p) = std::env::var_os("YDOTOOL_SOCKET") {
         let path = std::path::PathBuf::from(&p);
-        if !p.is_empty() && path.exists() {
+        if !p.is_empty() && socket_is_live(&path) {
             return Some(path);
         }
     }
@@ -1534,7 +1550,7 @@
     }
     candidates.push(std::path::PathBuf::from("/tmp/.ydotool_socket"));
     candidates.push(std::path::PathBuf::from("/run/ydotoold.socket"));
-    candidates.into_iter().find(|p| p.exists())
+    candidates.into_iter().find(|p| socket_is_live(p))
 }
 
